"""spec.py RPC-output wiring template for a semantic-layer data product.

Copy the wiring blocks below into your spec.py, filling in the infra-profile
service refs, data product name, and your real semantic model. This template is
the canonical reference for the exact call shapes; it matches the recipe in
SKILL.md (Steps 3 + 4) and mirrors the proven, deployed `pharma-subjects-demo`.

LAYOUT — keep every module FLAT at the DP root (siblings of spec.py):
    registry.py   tools.py   provision.py   transform.py   models.py   spec.py
Do NOT create a `transform/` subdir package — package-qualified imports
(`from transform.registry import REGISTRY`) need the DP root on sys.path, which
is absent in the rpc subprocess. Flat siblings import flat: `from registry
import REGISTRY`. provision.py (DDL) creates the table + view; transform.py
seeds the data.

Required imports (add to your spec.py):
    from nxd.spec import (
        Predicate,
        code,
        data_product,
        data_product_output,
        data_product_rpc_output,
        rpc_function, rpc_server, script, storage,
    )

The semantic library + sibling imports (add to your spec.py):
    from nxd.experimental.semantic import build_semantic_tools
    from registry import REGISTRY
    from tools import list_models, describe_model, run_semantic_query
    from transform import transform   # provision.py is referenced by name in .provision(script(...))
    from models import subjects_model   # your REAL semantic model

WIRING PATTERN — the only correct way to expose MCP tools in NXD:

    # build_semantic_tools(REGISTRY) is used ONLY for each tool's request/response
    # schema descriptors and description. The actual CALLABLE passed to code() is
    # the module-level function from tools.py — NOT `t.fn`, which is a closure
    # (build_semantic_tools.<locals>.list_models) that code()'s inspect.getsource
    # cannot locate. Passing code(t.fn) makes the pod fail to build.
    _tool_map = {t.name: t for t in build_semantic_tools(REGISTRY)}

    _rpc = data_product_rpc_output()
    for _fn, _name in [
        (list_models, "list_models"),
        (describe_model, "describe_model"),
        (run_semantic_query, "run_semantic_query"),
    ]:
        _t = _tool_map[_name]
        _rpc = _rpc.function(
            rpc_function(code(_fn), _t.request_model, _t.response_model)
            .description(_t.description)
        )
    _rpc = _rpc.port(
        "mcp-api",
        rpc_server("{{infra_profile_mcp_service_ref}}")
        .enable_endpoints()
        .mcp_path("/mcp"),
    )

    # Storage output port — its name is the provision/transform parameter name and
    # supplies the Snowflake connection the provision hook (to create the table +
    # view), the transform (to seed rows), and the tools (run_semantic_query, to
    # read it) use. Use a PLAIN storage(...) port and .promise() the REAL semantic
    # model — see the IMPORTANT note on the facade as_view path below.
    _storage = (
        data_product_output()
        # Promise the REAL semantic model (NOT a marker). The provision hook creates
        # the matching managed table and the transform seeds it, so the discover UI
        # surfaces its real attributes and glossary links.
        .promise(subjects_model)
        .port("snowflake", storage("{{infra_profile_snowflake_service_ref}}"))
    )

    spec = (
        data_product(
            name="{{dp_name}}",
            domain="{{domain}}",
            description="{{description}}",
            version="0.1.0",
            infra_profile="{{infra_profile}}",
        )
        # PROVISION (Phase A, before the transform): the @on_provision hook
        # (provision.py) creates the table STRUCTURE (CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS via
        # snowflake.full_table_name("<model>")) + the single-table <MODEL>_SEMANTIC
        # view — DDL ONLY. Placed IMMEDIATELY BEFORE .transform(). NO
        # .startup_timeout(...) here — the validator rejects it on .provision();
        # provision inherits the transform executor's startup budget.
        .provision(
            script("provision.py")
            .compute("{{infra_profile_compute_service_ref}}")
        )
        # TRANSFORM: seeds the ROWS (TRUNCATE TABLE IF EXISTS + write_pandas, NO
        # DDL) into the table the provision hook created, and bundles the sibling
        # registry.py / tools.py / provision.py modules into the image (the **/*.py
        # glob runs on the transform/compute path — .output(_rpc) alone ships only
        # the extracted tool scripts, so without a .transform(...) the pod dies
        # "ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'registry'"). The output-port
        # promise is verified AFTER the transform, so the rows are present in time.
        #
        # .startup_timeout(600) is a METHOD on the compute spec returned by
        # .compute(...). A cold compute boot connects to Snowflake + builds
        # snowflake-connector-python[pandas] + pandas, which blows past the default
        # → "Timeout waiting for execution to start" → DP Failed. 600s comfortably
        # covers a cold build and mesh-launch contention. (There is NO
        # provision_timeout_secs factory kwarg — that signature does not exist on
        # the installed wheel.)
        .transform(
            code(transform)
            .compute("{{infra_profile_compute_service_ref}}")
            .startup_timeout(600)
        )
        .output(_storage)
        .output(_rpc)
        # Glossary links surface the model + attribute terms in the discover UI.
        .link(Predicate.GlossaryTerm, "/data-product/{{domain}}/<glossary-dp>#/terms/<term>")
    )

SERVICE REF FORM:
    "/infra-profile/<INFRA_PROFILE_NAME>#/services/<service-name>"
    e.g. "/infra-profile/ecommerce-demo#/services/nxd-snowflake"

IMPORTANT:
- `data_product_rpc_output()` is the ONLY supported MCP delivery mechanism.
- Pass code(<module-level tools.py function>) — NEVER code(t.fn) over
  build_semantic_tools(...). Those are closures and code() cannot extract them.
- A `.transform(...)` is MANDATORY: it seeds the data AND bundles the sibling
  registry.py / tools.py / provision.py modules. `.output(_rpc)` alone ships only
  the extracted tool scripts.
- DDL IN @on_provision, DATA IN THE TRANSFORM. The @on_provision hook
  (provision.py, wired via `.provision(script("provision.py").compute(...))`
  IMMEDIATELY BEFORE `.transform(...)`) creates the table STRUCTURE
  (`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`) + the single-table `<MODEL>_SEMANTIC` view
  (`CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW`). The transform seeds only the ROWS
  (`TRUNCATE TABLE IF EXISTS` + write_pandas) — it NEVER issues DDL (a transform
  that re-provisions made DPs flap Started<->Failed). Both target
  `snowflake.full_table_name("<model>")` so produce-verification matches. Kernel
  order is provision -> transform -> promise verification, so the rows are present
  when the promise is checked. provision.py needs a trailing
  `if __name__ == "__main__":` guard (data_product.provision()), or the build
  raises a ValidationError.
- `.promise(<real_semantic_model>)` — promise the REAL model, never a marker.
- `.startup_timeout(600)` is a METHOD chained on the `.compute(...)` spec. There
  is NO `provision_timeout_secs` factory kwarg on `data_product(...)`. It goes on
  `.transform(...)` ONLY — the validator raises a ValidationError if chained on
  `.provision(...)`.
- USE THE PROVISION-HOOK/TRANSFORM SPLIT, NOT THE FACADE. Expose a PLAIN
  `storage(...)` port. Do NOT use the facade
  `storage(...).config(SnowflakeConfig().as_view(...))` path here: the facade
  `as_view` is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with `.transform()` — `nxd validate` raises
  `Facade view output(s) ... cannot be combined with .transform()` — and an rpc DP
  *needs* the transform to bundle registry.py/tools.py. (The facade pattern is fine
  for a pure-storage DP with no rpc tools; it is not an option here.)
- The view DDL must reference ONLY this DP's own tables. Never call the compiler's
  `native_semantic_view_ddl` / `plain_view_ddl` when the registry declares a
  cross-DP join — they emit a JOIN to another DP's table, so CREATE VIEW binds a
  missing object and the transform fails. Hand-author a single-table view.
- `rpc_function(code(fn), request_model, response_model)` requires ALL THREE
  positional arguments — omitting one raises a TypeError at spec-build time.
- `.description(t.description)` is chainable on rpc_function and sets the MCP
  tool description visible to agents.
- `.enable_endpoints()` publishes the HTTP+MCP endpoint on the rpc_server.
- `.mcp_path("/mcp")` sets the MCP mount path.
"""
